    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey i'm studying for a food chem test and i keep getting confused about the browning process. can u explain what the maillard reaction actually is and what temps i need to look out for to make sure it happens during baking?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Maillard reaction is a non-enzymatic browning process where reducing sugars react with amino acids (from proteins), producing hundreds of flavor and brown color compounds. It kicks in around 140°C (285°F) and accelerates up to roughly 180°C (355°F), though it can slowly proceed below these numbers with time. Don't confuse it with caramelization, which requires only sugar and starts at higher temps (typically 160°C+ depending on the sugar type). Lower moisture and surface dryness also speed it up, which is why bread crusts brown while the interior stays pale.",
      "source": "target",
